Clerk: The lift? Do you mean the elevator, ma''am?
Mia: Oh, yes! In Britain we usually call it the lift.
Clerk: I see. The elevator is just behind the shoe section.
Mia: Thank you. By the way, where do you sell winter boots?
Clerk: They are downstairs, on the first floor.
Mia: The first floor? But this is already the entrance floor.
Clerk: In American English, the first floor is the ground level.
Mia: Ah, that explains it. Thank you for being so patient!', 'Mia 问去顶楼的 lift(电梯)在哪;店员问她是不是指 elevator;Mia 说英国人叫 lift;店员指出电梯在鞋区后面;又说冬靴在 first floor(美式指底层),Mia 一度困惑,弄懂后道谢。', 'uk_female', '[{"q":"What is Mia looking for at first?","type":"choice","answer":"A","options":["The lift to the top floor.","A pair of gloves.","The way out of the shop.","A cup of coffee."]},{"q":"What does the American clerk call the lift?","type":"choice","answer":"B","options":["The subway.","The elevator.","The motorway.","The entrance."]},{"q":"Where is the elevator, according to the clerk?","type":"choice","answer":"A","options":["Just behind the shoe section.","Outside the building.","On the top floor.","Next to the boots."]},{"q":"Where are the winter boots sold?","type":"choice","answer":"B","options":["On the top floor.","Downstairs, on the first floor.","At the main entrance.","In another shop."]},{"q":"Why was Mia confused about the ''first floor''?","type":"choice","answer":"C","options":["The shop had no first floor.","The clerk spoke too fast.","In American English it means the ground level.","There was no lift at all."]}]'::jsonb, '[]'::jsonb, 'long', 'required1', 'U2', 'fltrp');
One way is to join two words, like ''home'' and ''work'' into ''homework''.
Another way is to add a prefix or a suffix.
Adding ''un-'' to ''happy'' gives the opposite, ''unhappy''.
We can also use one word as a different part of speech, such as to ''water'' the plants.', '英语用几种简单方式造新词:一是把两个词合在一起,如 home+work=homework;二是加前缀或后缀,如 happy 加 un- 变 unhappy(相反);三是把一个词用作不同词性,如把名词 water 用作动词''浇水''。', 'uk_female', '[{"q":"What is the passage mainly about?","type":"choice","answer":"D","options":["How to cook at home.","The history of dictionaries.","Travelling in Britain.","Ways of making new English words."]},{"q":"How is the word ''homework'' formed?","type":"choice","answer":"A","options":["By joining ''home'' and ''work''.","By adding a prefix to ''work''.","By shortening a longer word.","By changing its spelling."]},{"q":"What does adding ''un-'' to ''happy'' do?","type":"choice","answer":"B","options":["It makes the word a verb.","It gives the opposite meaning.","It makes the word longer in sound only.","It changes nothing."]},{"q":"Which is given as the third way to make words?","type":"choice","answer":"C","options":["Spelling words backwards.","Borrowing words from French.","Using one word as a different part of speech.","Drawing a picture."]},{"q":"Which example shows a word used as a verb?","type":"choice","answer":"D","options":["''Homework''.","''Unhappy''.","A prefix.","To ''water'' the plants."]}]'::jsonb, '[]'::jsonb, 'short', 'required1', 'U2', 'fltrp');
Sara: It means ''be right back''. He''ll return in a minute.
Leo: Oh, I thought it was the name of a website!
Sara: No, it''s just a short form people use to save time.
Leo: I see. And he often writes ''COZ'' too.
Sara: That simply means ''because''. It''s quick to type.
Leo: These short forms are clever, but a bit confusing at first.
Sara: True. Just be careful using them with teachers, who may not understand.', 'Leo 不懂网友打的 BRB;Sara 解释意思是''马上回来'';Leo 以为是网站名;Sara 说只是省时的缩写;Leo 又问 COZ;Sara 说意思是 because;两人说这些缩写聪明但起初令人困惑,对老师用要小心。', 'uk_female', '[{"q":"What does ''BRB'' mean?","type":"choice","answer":"A","options":["Be right back.","Be very busy.","Bring a book.","Buy red boots."]},{"q":"What did Leo first think ''BRB'' was?","type":"choice","answer":"B","options":["A kind of food.","The name of a website.","A spelling mistake.","A foreign city."]},{"q":"Why do people use these short forms?","type":"choice","answer":"C","options":["To make messages longer.","To confuse their friends.","To save time when typing.","To follow a school rule."]},{"q":"What does ''COZ'' stand for?","type":"choice","answer":"D","options":["Cannot.","Come on.","Of course.","Because."]},{"q":"What does Sara warn Leo about?","type":"choice","answer":"A","options":["Teachers may not understand these short forms.","The forms are against the law.","He must never type again.","The website will close soon."]}]'::jsonb, '[]'::jsonb, 'long', 'required1', 'U2', 'fltrp');
There is no ham in a hamburger and no egg in an eggplant.
We sculpt a sculpture, but we simply take a photo.
We feel seasick at sea, yet we are not homesick when we get home.
These surprises show how creative the language can be.', '英语学起来很有趣:hamburger 里没有 ham,eggplant 里没有 egg;我们 sculpt 出 sculpture,却是 take 一张 photo;在海上 seasick,回到家却不 homesick;这些惊喜说明这门语言很有创造力。', 'uk_female', '[{"q":"What is the passage mainly saying about English?","type":"choice","answer":"B","options":["It is the easiest language in the world.","It can be a funny and surprising language.","It has very few words.","It is spoken only in Britain."]},{"q":"What does the speaker say about a hamburger?","type":"choice","answer":"C","options":["It is always made with egg.","It contains pineapple.","There is no ham in it.","It is a kind of sculpture."]},{"q":"How do we describe taking a picture?","type":"choice","answer":"D","options":["We ''sculpt'' a photo.","We ''paint'' a photo.","We ''build'' a photo.","We ''take'' a photo."]},{"q":"When do people feel seasick?","type":"choice","answer":"A","options":["At sea.","At home.","In the air.","On a train."]},{"q":"What do these surprises show about English?","type":"choice","answer":"B","options":["It is completely useless.","It can be very creative.","It never changes.","It was made by machines."]}]'::jsonb, '[]'::jsonb, 'short', 'required1', 'U2', 'fltrp');
Ann: A frog in her throat? Did she really swallow a frog?
Tom: No, no. It just means she could hardly speak clearly.
Ann: Oh! So her throat was dry and her voice was weak.
Tom: Exactly. It''s a funny English expression.
Ann: English is full of these tricky expressions.
Tom: It is. At first they sound very strange.
Ann: But once you learn the real meaning, they''re quite fun.', 'Tom 说老师 Maggie 今天不能上课,因为''喉咙里有只青蛙'';Ann 以为她真吞了青蛙;Tom 解释那只是说她几乎说不清话;Ann 明白是嗓子干、声音弱;两人感叹英语满是这种棘手却有趣的表达。', 'uk_female', '[{"q":"Why couldn''t Maggie teach that day?","type":"choice","answer":"C","options":["She was on holiday.","She had lost her books.","She had a frog in her throat.","She had moved to another city."]},{"q":"What did Ann think at first?","type":"choice","answer":"D","options":["That Maggie was joking.","That Maggie had a pet frog.","That Maggie was angry.","That Maggie had really swallowed a frog."]},{"q":"What does the expression really mean?","type":"choice","answer":"A","options":["She could hardly speak clearly.","She had eaten too much.","She felt very happy.","She had caught a real frog."]},{"q":"What do Tom and Ann say about such expressions?","type":"choice","answer":"B","options":["They are easy from the start.","They sound strange at first.","They are never used in English.","They are completely useless."]},{"q":"How does Ann feel once she knows the meaning?","type":"choice","answer":"C","options":["She finds them boring.","She wants to stop learning English.","She finds them quite fun.","She is still confused."]}]'::jsonb, '[]'::jsonb, 'long', 'required1', 'U2', 'fltrp');
Short forms and emojis can be quick and clear.
But before you use them, stop and think about your reader.
A teacher may not know what you intend to say.
Make sure your words really suit the situation.', '我们网上用的语言常和其他英语不同;缩写和表情符号又快又清楚;但用之前先想想读者;老师也许不懂你想表达什么;要确保你的用词真正适合当下情境。', 'uk_female', '[{"q":"What is the main advice in the passage?","type":"choice","answer":"D","options":["Never write any messages.","Always use as many emojis as possible.","Only write to teachers.","Choose online words that suit your reader and situation."]},{"q":"How does the speaker describe short forms and emojis?","type":"choice","answer":"A","options":["They can be quick and clear.","They are always wrong.","They take a very long time.","They mean nothing at all."]},{"q":"What should you do before using them?","type":"choice","answer":"B","options":["Send them to everyone at once.","Stop and think about your reader.","Forget about the meaning.","Use only Chinese instead."]},{"q":"Who might not understand your short forms?","type":"choice","answer":"C","options":["A close friend.","A classmate online.","A teacher.","A person your own age."]},{"q":"What should your words ''really suit''?","type":"choice","answer":"D","options":["The weather.","The price.","The colour.","The situation."]}]'::jsonb, '[]'::jsonb, 'short', 'required1', 'U2', 'fltrp');

